            <ArticleTitle>Fulminant type 1 diabetes mellitus.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2012-006560</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2012006560</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present a fatal case of fulminant type 1 diabetes mellitus that was initially diagnosed as upper respiratory tract infection based on pharyngeal redness at a clinic. However, the patient then went into cardiopulmonary arrest, and was transferred to our hospital for treatment. Testing revealed very high levels of blood glucose (86.9 mmol/l), urinary glucose (2+) and ketones (4+). His glycosylated haemoglobin level was almost normal (6.2%; normal &lt;6.2%). Autopsy revealed marked depletion and atrophy of the islets of Langerhans.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
